Function References
A Function Reference is a method of passing an already existing function as an argument instead of a new Lambda Everytime.
Syntax:
:: (double colon) + function name
It makes the code shorter, cleaner and more readable.
Top-Level Function Refers to a standalone function that exist outside any class.
Syntax:
::functionName
Method Reference refers to a function that belongs to an object instance.
Syntax:
instance::methodName
Extension Function Reference refers to a function defined with an extension reciever.
Syntax:
receiverType::extensionFunctionName
Bound Extension Function Reference is used when we want to bind the receiver ahead of time.
Syntax:
instance::extensionFunctionName
Constructor Reference references Class Constructor directly.
Syntax:
::ClassName
Eg:
val makeZero = ::Complex // Type: (Double, Double) -> Complex
val z = makeZero(0.0, 0.0)
println(z) // Complex(real=0.0, imaginary=0.0)
Function references do not support default parameters directly. If your function has defaults, wrap it in a lambda if you need them.